{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261118T201206Z-ea581870", "ts": "2026-11-18T20:18:42Z", "actor": {"worktree": ".claude/worktrees/household", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "fix/household-mixed-paces", "build": "3682719168c6"}, "body": {"base_commit": "3c5b744f3557e3358f101fea4051b00765118949", "base_tree": "5da22dd3f906fe45fd9bd200b939cb1fad05dabf", "head_tree": "b3665133f0aeb0933a8a45876f450e06e1807828", "head_commit": "752f00d4e92214c63a34afacad63e69fa5288c90", "dispatch_commit": "752f00d4e92214c63a34afacad63e69fa5288c90", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_major_2_feed.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_major_2_feed.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The strict xfail's 'the clash is named' assertion is satisfied by the unavoidable kept-slot clash, so its alarm depends on build order", "recommendation": "Instance. In test_three_walls_a_pass_cannot_keep_apart_though_some_schedule_would, `assert built[\"n2\"].clashes != ()` comes before the apartness assertion. Under xfail any failure counts as expected, so today that assertion can never fail visibly. It also does not test what the docstring says. With offsets {a: 1100, n1: 4662, n2: 6942}, n1 and n2 both show `w` now (n1 [-1062s, +2538s), n2 [-3342s, +258s)). The pass order is a, n2, n1 (one pace; free counts a=2, n2=2, n1=3). So n2 is built with n1 movable, and its kept slot names the unavoidable kept-against-kept clash with n1. `clashes != ()` holds whether or not the avoidable clash at a's turn to `y` is named. A future pass that keeps these walls apart but builds n1 before n2 leaves n2.clashes empty. The first assertion then fails, the test stays 'xfailed', and strict never fires. That breaks the docstring's promise that 'a pass that keeps these apart says so by failing here'. The fix: make apartness the xfail's only condition. If naming the avoidable clash is meant to be pinned, assert that specific clash (n2, its slot starting where a turns to `y`) in a separate non-xfail test. The learnings rule 'When a test, lint or check is new or rewritten, watch it fail once against the unfixed code or a re-break' applies.", "files": ["arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "_apart_after_now ignores each wall's slot now against the other's slots to come, which is wider than the only unavoidable overlap", "recommendation": "Instance; one helper fixes all its callers. `_apart_after_now` skips a slot covering now on either side, so it never compares one wall's kept slot with the other wall's slots to come. The only overlap a pass cannot avoid is kept slot against kept slot. A kept slot against a later-built wall's future slot can be avoided, because the later wall sees the kept slot (whole, or as the slot now of a movable wall) and has a second work. The new property tests rely on this helper: case A, the two-wall 'never clash when one pace divides the other' test, case B, the slower-neighbours line-up and the one-work hall. A regression where a wall built later steps onto a neighbour's kept work would pass all of them. The schedule.py docstring's 'It keeps two walls apart whenever ...' and the change record's clash counts both rest on this weaker check. The service-level sibling in test_major_2_feed.py (case B) excludes only slots on the subject side and checks both directions, so in effect it excludes only kept-against-kept. Narrow the helper the same way: skip a pair only when both slots cover now.", "files": ["arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The service's lining-up with a neighbour built earlier (two or more walls ahead of the republished one) is never exercised through _write_v2", "recommendation": "In `_rebuild_neighbours`, the `with_wall not in built` branch reads the align target's feed back from disk. It runs only when a step's `align_with` is another neighbour, which needs at least two walls ordered before the republished one. Every service test in test_major_2_feed.py has at most one: case A, the refused slower study, and the build-order test have one, and case B at one pace has none. The two-slower-neighbours household is tested only in test_schedule.py, through `_household_pass`, a test-side copy of `_write_v2`'s choreography. Mutating `with_wall = step.align_with or wall_id` to `with_wall = wall_id` would leave every test green. The learnings rule 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value' applies. Add one service test with the hall (900s, {a,b,c}), a kitchen (hourly, {a,b}) and a study (two-hourly, {a,b}), asserting the kitchen and the study stay apart after now. Class: any choreography branch that only the `_household_pass` copy exercises. Search the copy for branches with no service-level counterpart.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_major_2_feed.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The pass's build sequence lives in two copies, and the property tests run the test-side copy", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because only the ordering (`household_pass`) went into the pure module. The sequence that is the actual fix went into `DisplayService._write_v2` / `_rebuild_neighbours`, mixed in with the I/O: build the republished wall provisionally, build the walls ahead against its first slot alone (`_Beside.pending`), build it again with `Keep` and `also={first.work_id}`, then rebuild the walls after it. `test_schedule.py::_household_pass` re-implements that sequence step for step (its docstring says 'as `DisplayService._write_v2` does'). Every pure-pass property runs against that copy: case A, case B, the divides-the-other property, the two-slower-neighbours property and the strict xfail. So does the change record's measured clash drop (258-273 to 54-76 of 300). The consequence: someone who edits the sequence in `_write_v2` (drops the `pending` stand-in, the `also` allowance, or the re-read before the second build) gets a green run from those 100-200-example properties, and only the three service tests in `test_major_2_feed.py` exercise the real path. That cuts against the module's own **Pure** paragraph: a function with no I/O is what lets the suite state the household rule as a property. Remedy: have the pure module own the whole sequence. One way is for `household_pass` to return the full plan, including the republished wall's provisional build, the first-slot stand-in for the steps ahead of it and its second build with `Keep`, so that `_write_v2` and the test helper are each a single loop over steps with no sequencing logic of their own. Then delete the copy in the helper. Learnings: 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller' - here the properties test a re-implementation of the caller.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Slower neighbours are now written before the republished wall, and a failure in its second build or write leaves them scheduled against a slot that was never published, with no clash named", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because the new order rebuilds and writes the walls in steps[:mine] (via _rebuild_neighbours -> _rebuild_beside -> _republish -> _write_v2) before the republished wall's second build_schedule and _publish_feed run. Before this change the republished wall was written first, so a failure there touched no neighbour. Now, if that second build raises (schedule.build's ValueErrors) or _publish_feed raises (the Library call in _control_layer, or write_atomically), the exception propagates. The neighbours already on disk were built with published[wall_id] = (first,) in place of the wall's real feed, so their slots to come can overlap the old feed that stays published. Nothing logs it: clashes are computed only at build time, against the pending slot. The schedule module calls a clash nobody hears about the silent failure. The same window lets a non-ServiceError from a slower neighbour's rebuild abort the republished wall's own act. The comment inside _rebuild_neighbours, 'This wall's own act succeeded; the neighbour goes on...', is false for the pre-phase call. The cheapest fix is a try/except around the second build and _publish_feed when mine > 0. It logs at warning which walls were rewritten against an unpublished first slot, then re-raises. The other option is to hold the steps[:mine] schedules in memory and write them after the republished wall. Either way, a test should make _publish_feed raise for the republished wall and assert the warning.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The plan's 'comes back as proposed' for the general guarantee has no record, while the change record closes #374", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The plan's Goals say a general guarantee that needs a search over schedules 'comes back as **proposed**, not built'. The work found exactly that residue: case B still clashes on some offsets (3 of 200), pinned by the strict xfail test_three_walls_a_pass_cannot_keep_apart_though_some_schedule_would, and the module docstring now says 'Ruling that out needs the walls' schedules searched together'. But no proposal is recorded anywhere in the repo: nothing in .prawduct/backlog.md, and the change record names no follow-up issue. Meanwhile its `closes: [brookstalley/arrt#374]` closes the only tracker for the issue's direction, 'the household rule holds whenever some schedule satisfies it'. The consequence is that the owner sees #374 closed and never gets the proposal the plan promised to rule on. The remaining requirement then lives only in a docstring and an xfail reason. I could not check GitHub. Either file the proposal and name it in the change record and the xfail reason, or record why #374 closing is the intended outcome.", "files": ["arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py"], "fid": "R-6"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 6, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-artifact reconciliation: the plan has no governed_by field, though the product carries ## Direction sections", "recommendation": "Record-lint reports governed-by-gap 0, and the plan cites none. I checked the rulings the plan carries against the diff. They hold. The slot now is kept on every wall: Keep is used for each step, and the republished wall's second build keeps `first`. A kept slot lingers by less than one interval (`_lengthened`'s 2x cap is unchanged). Each wall is written once, which test_a_slower_neighbour_is_built_against_the_hall_s_first_slot_alone_and_each_wall_is_written_once asserts. No architecture.md Direction norm covers the schedule's internals, and the feed's shape is unchanged. Running `prawduct-hook jurisdiction` to seed the field would make that disposition explicit.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The plan's 'rebuilds each wall at most once per write' became 'written at most once': the republished wall is now built twice", "recommendation": "When a wall goes ahead of it, `_write_v2` builds the republished wall twice in memory and publishes once. The change record and the docstring restate the plan's invariant as 'written'. The intent holds (no write cascade, no ping-pong), so no edit is wanted. The wording moved without a note in the plan.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The joint-search guarantee the plan says 'comes back as proposed' is named only in the docstring and the xfail, while #374 is closed", "recommendation": "The remainder is explicitly descoped, not dropped: schedule.py's 'What a pass cannot do' and the strict xfail both name it. The plan asks for it to come back as a proposal, and the change record's `closes: [#374]` closes the issue that held the direction. No proposal appears in the diff. I did not check the GitHub backlog. Confirm one exists, or the direction lives nowhere a backlog read finds it.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Plan Done-when says existing schedule tests pass 'unchanged', but the `_household_pass` helper three #350 tests depend on was rewritten", "recommendation": "No assertion was weakened. The bodies of test_two_walls_locked_on_one_work_each_are_unlocked_by_one_pass, test_repeated_passes_from_either_wall_never_lock_again and test_after_a_pass_every_overlap_between_walls_is_a_named_clash are untouched. They now run the new choreography, and the target's first build now uses movable_walls rather than every neighbour. The commit message lists only the test_major_2_feed fixture change as an existing test whose setup changed. Informational.", "files": ["arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "household_pass takes movable_walls's answer as a parameter and re-derives its 'fixed' measure", "recommendation": "The `fixed` comprehension in `household_pass` (schedule.py ~L287) is a verbatim copy of the one in `movable_walls` (~L236). Both callers (`_write_v2` and the test helper) call `movable_walls(wall_id, neighbours, works)` with the same arguments just before passing its result in. Two options: have `household_pass` call `movable_walls` itself and expose the result (the caller still needs it for the provisional build), or extract one shared helper for 'works to go to'. Either way the order and the movable set cannot be computed on different measures. This matters because `_write_v2`'s `schedule.slots[0]` relies on `free[wall_id] >= 2`.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "_rebuild_neighbours and _write_v2 carry generality and a repeated read they do not use", "recommendation": "`_rebuild_neighbours(built: Mapping[str, Schedule])` is only ever passed `{wall_id: schedule}`, and `step.align_with or wall_id` has an unreachable fallback, because the republished wall's own step (the only one with `align_with=None`) is never in the slices passed in. In `_write_v2`, the all-walls feed read is written out twice. The `scheduled` closure also depends on `published` being rebound after the closure is defined (late binding), which is easy to break by changing the rebinding to a local. A single `schedule: Schedule` parameter, a `_published_slots()` helper and passing `published` explicitly would each remove one of these.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: the oracle set was covered and nothing cleared a bar", "recommendation": "I rated .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md and .prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md against the three bars. Checked against the diff, the change record's claims all hold: the pass order, building against the first slot, the republished wall built twice and written once, each wall written at most once (steps[:mine] and steps[mine+1:] are disjoint and exclude the wall), and a refused neighbour keeping its schedule (tested). Two small imprecisions clear no bar and are not findings. The order list omits the rule that puts a republished wall with at most one work to go to first; the docstring and a parametrized test carry it. The title says walls at mixed paces 'keep apart', and the body qualifies that. The plan's chunk 02 says existing tests pass 'unchanged', but one existing test's setup gained a work `z`. Commit 3ee64b39's message records why, and the test docstring does too. No edit is wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The measured clash rates in the change record are not reproducible from the suite", "recommendation": "The learnings rule 'When you record something as verified, name what was measured (fields, cases, N) and keep the check in the suite if a change could void it' applies. The change record names N: 258-273 of 300 down to 54-76 of 300 for three walls at mixed paces, and 36 of 200 down to 3 of 200 for case B. The random-household measurement behind those numbers is not in the repo. The suite's property tests pin the two-wall guarantee, not these rates, so a later change to household_pass could void the figures silently. Nothing reads them and no action follows from them, so this stays an observation. A small script under arrt/tools/ that reproduces them would let the next change to the pass re-measure.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md"], "oid": "O-8"}], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": "household-mixed-paces", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": "3c5b744f3557e3358f101fea4051b00765118949", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-18T20:12:07Z"}}
